WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.840
Sujata Day, Actor, Writer/Director:&nbsp;
Issa Rae just taught me to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:09.200
be true to my point of view and&nbsp;
get it out there no matter what,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.640
no matter how many rejections you&nbsp;
get, no matter how many no's you get.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:16.160
Film Courage: How did you meet Issa Rae?

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:32.080
Sujata Day: I met Issa Rae on Twitter this was 10&nbsp;
years plus a couple months ago and I had just left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:38.720
Facebook because I just thought Facebook was so&nbsp;
dramatic and there was just too much going on with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:42.080
having your elementary school friends on there&nbsp;
with your middle school friends with your high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:46.080
school friends with your college friends&nbsp;
with your family. I was like I need to not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:51.200
be part of this chatter so I left Facebook&nbsp;
before it was even cool to leave Facebook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:58.240
and as I left it I said to myself I was panicking&nbsp;
a little bit and I said well I need some kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:03.840
social media what's it gonna be and then I saw&nbsp;
that Twitter was happening and not happening like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:10.000
today but it was it was a newish platform&nbsp;
so I was like okay I'm gonna make a Twitter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:18.160
and then I got on Twitter and I started following&nbsp;
this this person Joshua at Film TV Diversity and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:25.760
he would tweet out different writers and producers&nbsp;
and directors and creators to follow so I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:30.160
just click follow on every single one of them&nbsp;
because I didn't know what I was doing on Twitter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:35.520
and and there was always really great advice&nbsp;
coming from Twitter from the people I follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:43.680
in terms of filmmaking and then she had tweeted&nbsp;
out follow Issa Rae she makes web series and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:50.000
said great followed her she followed me right&nbsp;
back and then a couple days later she Tweeted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.120
out I'm looking for a mixed looking girl to&nbsp;
play my best friend in my next web series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:02.080
and I dm'ed her and I said hey I'm not mixed but&nbsp;
this is what I look like here's a picture of me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:09.520
can I audition anyway and she said yeah of course&nbsp;
come on down and she gave me the address and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:16.400
pull up to the house this is a house in a nice&nbsp;
neighborhood but regardless a house and for any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:21.840
actors watching out there I don't recommend you&nbsp;
go to a house for an audition that's super shady&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:30.160
you want to drive away I did not drive away I&nbsp;
was about to drive away but then I saw Issa come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:36.160
out and she was talking on her cell phone and I&nbsp;
was like oh it's fine it's a girl it's a woman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:43.040
still not the right attitude to have so I went&nbsp;
into the house did the audition it was super chill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:47.520
I was there for about five minutes and then&nbsp;
I left and then a couple hours later she said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:56.560
you booked the roll and I said okay cool and&nbsp;
then a couple weeks later I went to Issa's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:03.040
dad's doctor's office in Inglewood which where&nbsp;
we would be shooting Awkward Black girl and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:09.280
just shot a very quick 20-minute hallway scene&nbsp;
that I believe is in episode three of awkward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:16.080
black girl where my character is introduced&nbsp;
and it was just me isa and her brother and her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:21.680
brother was running the camera and the sound was&nbsp;
definitely attached to the microphone was attached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:27.280
to the camera so there was no separate sound&nbsp;
system we had to come hair and makeup ready and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:34.080
like I said it took 20 minutes and then she&nbsp;
isa said thank you so much and then I left and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:41.680
I asked myself what did I get myself into what&nbsp;
is this web series like nobody knew what web&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:50.160
series was at the time and that's how I met Issa&nbsp;
Rae that's a great story you'd been acting what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:58.240
eight years prior to doing Awkward Black Girl well&nbsp;
I've I would say I've been acting since I was like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:05.360
five or six just from back home just putting on my&nbsp;
own shows putting on my own performances and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.080
doing plays in elementary&nbsp;
middle school and then doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:18.160
musicals and being in the chorus in middle&nbsp;
school and high school and so I've been acting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:25.040
I would say pretty much my whole life yeah you&nbsp;
did the web series for free correct yes I did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:30.160
and that's an interesting decision which I think&nbsp;
at the time I was doing a lot of stuff for free so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:35.600
like I said I was auditioning for&nbsp;
AFI films I was auditioning for USC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:42.000
films and ucla films and most of the time those&nbsp;
films didn't even get finished and and that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:48.800
the tragedy of it all because I didn't mind doing&nbsp;
working for free because it was always really fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:56.720
but I believe that a lot of the time&nbsp;
the filmmakers came up against wanting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:03.360
their project to be perfect and then going&nbsp;
through the post-production process and realizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:10.080
that it wasn't what they had envisioned and then&nbsp;
completely throwing it out and I believe that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:17.680
the wrong attitude to have as a filmmaker&nbsp;
as a creator I truly believe that you should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:25.520
finish stuff and also put it out there and also&nbsp;
submit it to festivals and even if it's not what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:31.040
you thought it was going to be it could still&nbsp;
be accepted as something else and so that's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:36.560
exciting part of it even if you have something&nbsp;
that may not have the best production quality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:41.920
or the best acting or the best sound just&nbsp;
put it out there and guess what you're gonna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:48.560
learn from that mistake and you're not&nbsp;
gonna make that mistake again and your next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:54.800
thing or project will be ten times better oh and&nbsp;
then you just keep working you just gotta keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:01.440
making stuff and putting it out and then&nbsp;
make the next thing so something that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:06.880
like to point out is if you watch the first two&nbsp;
or so episodes of Awkward Black Girl on youtube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:14.880
the production quality isn't great the sound&nbsp;
isn't great but there's something there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:19.120
there's something there that makes you&nbsp;
keep coming back and then you notice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:27.680
as those season one episodes go on oh they I think&nbsp;
they got lighting I think they might have gotten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:34.240
a microphone they might have gotten a sound person&nbsp;
oh their their wardrobe looks a little bit better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:42.800
so I love Awkward Black Girl in that it it is&nbsp;
on Youtube and you can watch the progression&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:50.400
of how it just keeps getting better and better&nbsp;
and and I think that was you know when I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:56.640
doing free projects I was just excited to be&nbsp;
meeting people and working with new people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:03.600
and once again just be surrounded by creativity&nbsp;
what do you think made that show so popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:10.880
what made Awkward Black Girl popular was that&nbsp;
it reached an audience that was being ignored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:20.400
by Hollywood and it was black women that&nbsp;
were not used to seeing themselves on screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:27.680
and that's that's what was really exciting about&nbsp;
being a part of that journey and watching as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:36.560
the episodes came out one by one watching the&nbsp;
the view count keep growing and it turned into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:43.360
snowball and and we were getting comments we were&nbsp;
getting dms we were getting messages from people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:50.000
oh my god I've never seen myself on screen before&nbsp;
this is me or do you have a camera in my home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:55.680
are you watching my life are you watching my&nbsp;
relationships are you watching my friendships&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:01.840
and that was the most exciting part of it&nbsp;
because like I said Hollywood has a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:09.840
of taking a group of people and making the same&nbsp;
story about them over and over and over again and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:16.880
